nguage in  product development because the graph  based system resembles my natural mental  model for relationships between real  world things and maybe someone who are  looking at this and thinking how is this  different from a database model the  sir has to do with what we're focusing  on when we're designing each when  designing a database structure we care  about things like consistency redundancy  and efficiency on the back end when  designing a graphic UL schema we care  about the needs of our front-end client  specifically when writing the schema  it's definitely tempting to just  recreate a 101 in mapping to your  underlying database setup or to your  existing REST API but by doing so you're  introducing unnecessary complexity on  the client side let's look at an example  let's say this is the UI we're building  floor please don't judge my design  skills too harshly I get mistaken for a  designer all the time but I definitely  don't have those skills we want to  display information about an interview  loop namely we want to display the  candidate name the job that they applied  for and then a list of their interviews  including their interviewers name and  their feedback what would that look like  if our graph UL types were just mappings  to our database we'll start from the  interview loop on the left is our domain  style schema definition and on the right  is our database style schema definition  you'll see that the difference is that  the relationships to other types are  represented by string IDs instead of by  the actual type so assuming we're gonna  use our database style schema how does  this affect the way that we write our  client code buckle in this is what a  query in graph QL looks like in this one  we're giving some interview loop ID and  asking for the IDS of the candidate the  job requisition and all the interviews  on the loop we stored those IDs and then  we kick off some other queries we do  three more queries to get the candidate  name the job requisition title and then  all the data that we need for each of  the interviews and we're still not done  because for each of those interviews we  also need to get the interviewers name  and then there's a bunch of code that we  still have to write to parse this data  and map it into the right format  remember this is more complex than it  needs to be because of our decision to  make our graph QL schema map to our  databases what would this look like if  we design our schema to match our mental  model instead if we wrote our schema  tonight  our mental model we can get exactly the  data that we want in the shape that we  want with just one request being able to  write queries like this simplifies our  work on the client by a lot and if we  ran this query this would be the data  that we get back we have our candidate  Barack Obama who applied for a product  engineer role and was interviewed by  Michelle Obama which is not a conflict  of interest at all don't worry after you  get some initial ideas down it's time to  work together with your team to make it  good and for this part to go well  everyone has to have a shared  understanding of your domain and even  more importantly an established shared  language to talk about it with why is  this part so important without a shared  language collaboration between teams  within a company is really inefficient  you're gonna mix business jargon with  technical jargon and everyone is just  going to be constantly talking past each  other without even realizing it having a  shared language removes a large chunk of  ambiguity and misinterpretation and it  ultimately saves you time and especially  when we're building for complex domains  we need to iterate quickly to keep up  with the ever-changing nature of our  domain so we need those tight feedback  loops so I take my text back and I set  up a meeting actually I'm gonna call it  a working session after Anna's talk  yesterday I really like that I set up a  working session with maybe one other  engineer a p.m. a designer and a domain  expert so my last job our domain experts  were longtime recruiters and recruiting  managers these are the people who have  the most on-the-ground realistic  knowledge of your domain so you're  really doing yourself a disservice if  you don't include them early in your  development process I'm gonna say it  again because I think it's really  important talk to your domain experts  like think right now who are your domain  experts for the area that you work in  and how can you include them in your  development process more where were we  okay we're in the working session we're  collaborating on our proposed changes  and in the session we talked about a lot  of things but I wanted to share some  concrete tips for building and  maintaining a shared vocabulary or as  it's called in domain driven development  you  kotas language first ensure that you  only use one word for specific concept  don't let domain experts and developers  have two words that address the same  thing second try to stay away from  overloaded technical terms like service  policy manager etc in your API layer  words in your API layer should  correspond to concepts in your domain  third create and maintain a glossary of  terms with explicit definitions this is  something that you get basically for  free with graph key well it's really  cool and I'll show you how in a bit use  terms from your shared language  everywhere namespaces classes filenames  properties method names you should also  be able to find the same language that  you use in your code in your support  articles in your internal update emails  in your product specs this is how you  keep a shared language alive and useful  by using it and then as you gain a  deeper understanding of your business  domain your shared language will evolve  as well periodically refactor your code  base to reflect this this is also  something that's much easier to do in a  typed and well-documented code base like  one that uses graph QL and I know that  this sounds like a lot of work but it's  necessary work because there is always  going to be some underlying model and if  you're not putting conscious effort into  building a useful and evolvable model  then you're probably modeling garbage by  accident this is the kind of upfront  work that makes sure that you're solving  the right problem that you have the  correct assumptions about your domain  and that you're not making some  incorrect assumption into your model  that's going to give you headaches for  years to come another strategy in DDD is  separating your business logic from your  infrastructure logic and if you get  nothing else from this talk here's a  life hack for separating egg yolks  and why is it important to keep these  things separate not the egg yolks I mean  the different logic types over time your  product priorities change or your  understanding of your product domain  changes and so your product itself  changes there's a regular cadence within  which you add new features and whatnot  at the same time as your company grows  and it grows its user base you probably  also will need to make some  infrastructure changes to accommodate  that but those are two separate things  right you're changing product needs and  then you're changing scale needs a lot  of complexity in applications comes from  these two things being entangled when we  want them to be isolated so that they  can grow and change at their own pace  remember our good old schema graph QL  has a special type called query this is  a special type because it defines the  entry point for all of your data for  every type in our schema and for every  field on every type we have to teach  graph QL where that data actually lives  and how to get it we do this in the  resolvers and the resolvers are  functions that tell graph QL where and  how to resolve the types you've defined  this is where you'd write the code that  accesses your data source whatever it  may be let's look at this example  I wrote a MongoDB example but remember  you can put logic to ask us anything in  the resolvers in this example we have a  query called all interviews that returns  you guessed it all the interviews from a  MongoDB document we also have a query  called interviews by candidate that lets  you filter the interviews using a  candidate ID oh by the way if you like  this editor theme amy webelo made it  it's called fairy floss check it out as  I said before a few times in domain  driven development it's really important  for the whole team to have a shared  understanding of the business domain and  a shared language to use when talking  about it so you need to document  everything and I know that everyone says  this in like this really aspirational  way but graphic you graph to out really  does make this much easier because of  the type system your domain is  practically self documenting there's  this really cool tool  video playing yeah no is it yeah there's  this really cool tool called graphical  it's a sandbox for your graph QL schema  and here I have a video of graphical  over the github graph QL API if you want  to try out graph QL this is a really  good place to get started you can write  and run arbitrary queries and get real  data back and my favorite part is the  documentation Explorer on the right  where you can navigate and search  through every type that you've defined  in your schema including descriptions  for every type a protip is you can make  descriptions a required field for any  new type in your schema and you've got  built in documentation that way let's do  a recap at the beginning of the talk I  said that graph QL gives product team  superpowers by letting them focus on  their domain rather than their data and  we've learned that this domain focused  way of structuring your work is called  domain driven development how our graph  QL and domain driven development tied  together one it's impossible for a  schema designed to be an afterthought  because for anything to work in your API  you have to define it in your schema  first and this forces you to think  upfront about your schema and your  domain graph based modeling feels really  natural and speeds up development on the  client side the schema definition  reduces communication errors between  teams because everyone is on the same  page about what terms mean and what  relationships between types look like  you get built-in separation of concerns  between your domain and your infra logic  because of the resolvers and you get  really nice documentation with very  little extra effort because of the type  system and the awesome tooling that's  available in the graph QL ecosystem and  after all of this maybe some of y'all  are thinking it sounds like a lot of  work and a lot of meetings and first of  all yeah if you're not working in a  complex problem domain this is  definitely overkill but otherwise  personally getting to work more  collaboratively is one of the things  that I love about working with graphs  well and following DDD it's exposed me  to new domains and new opportunities too  about those domains so I got to  understand the entire recruiting  pipeline very in-depth and on my current  job I've gotten to learn a bunch about  political organizing how nonprofits run  and a ton about text messages as a  platform I also strongly believe that  the best product engineering teams don't  just use the most cutting-edge tech or  focus on building pristine code bases  they also think and care deeply about  their products their users and their  domain if you want to learn more about  DDD Eric Evans's domain driven design is  the canonical text and I also use  patterns principles and practices of  domain driven design aka the PPP of DDD  and if you'd like to hear more about  graph QL and domain driven design  specifically this talk by mark Andre  zero from graph QL Summit last year is  really good and also talks about it and  finally Emily Griffin made those  adorable illustrations you can find her  on Twitter at Emily with curls  you should totally Commission her to do  art for your next talk so that's it  thanks everyone  [Applause]  you
 

